messages:
amy: quick question — trying to ship one npm package that serves Node CJS, Node ESM, and an edge-targeted build (vercel/bun/deno). types are blowing up when users consume the edge entry. anyone wrestled this?
jordan: yup. the hard part is TS type resolution vs conditional exports. TS doesn't understand conditional exports for types the same way node does. :grimacing:
sasha: also edge runtimes can't use node-only libs, so you often need separate build outputs (cjs/esm/edge) and proper export conditions. keep the runtime surface minimal for edge (no fs, no crypto-node).
amy: here's what I tried in package.json (simplified):
```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "node": {
        "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
        "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
      },
      "edge": "./dist/edge/index.mjs",
      "default": "./dist/esm/index.mjs"
    },
    "./package.json": "./package.json"
  },
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
}
```
amy: TS still resolves to the esm build path for some consumers, and others (older bundlers) pick the cjs one. types bundle points at dist/index.d.ts but the declarations reference node-only types sometimes.
mike: pattern that worked for us: generate a unified .d.ts that uses conditional type shims (avoid node global types in edge-facing exports) and publish a small runtime shim for edge that re-exports the same TS types. also use `typesVersions` to help old TS tooling: 
```json
"typesVersions": {"*": {"*": ["dist/*"]}}
```
not perfect but helps.
jordan: another trick — keep types entry unconditional ("types": "dist/index.d.ts") but within the d.ts avoid `import 'node:fs'` types. If you need node-only helpers, split them into `node-only.d.ts` and don't re-export them from the edge entry.
sasha: fwiw, for consumers on Deno/Bun we also expose a tiny `edge` export name so they can import like `import X from 'pkg/edge'` when they explicitly want edge semantics. that avoids relying on the node/edge conditional resolver behavior.
amy: re: publishing flow — any gotchas for local testing? tarball installs / pnpm link behave differently with conditional exports.
mike: TIL: `npm pack` preserves exports mapping but some bundlers (parcel/older webpack) ignore `exports`. in those cases `main` / `module` fallback matters. consider including a `module` field for older tooling.
jordan: and bump the package's "engines"/"exports" guidance in README so integrators know the supported resolution strategy. clear docs reduce a ton of friction.
amy: thanks all. one more question — auth/retry semantics in edge vs node: should SDK surface differ? fetch vs node http error shapes mean retries can see different codes.
sasha: yes, edge fetch has opaque errors; we ended up normalizing in the SDK core: translate fetch/network failures to a consistent error shape (code + retriable flag). that lets top-level retry logic be identical across runtimes. pseudocode: 
```js
// runtime-shims/normalize-error.js
export function normalize(err) {
  if (err instanceof TypeError) return { code: 'FETCH_ERR', retriable: true }
  if (err.code === 'ECONNRESET') return { code: 'E_CONN_RESET', retriable: true }
  return { code: 'UNKNOWN', retriable: false }
}
```
mike: also be careful with exponential backoff in edge — some environments have strict compute windows (serverless). tuned defaults for edge: fewer retries, shorter overall timeout.
jordan: +1 on shorter retry horizon for edge, and make it configurable per-client so Dedicated/Hosted customers can tune.
amy: actionable items: 1) try splitting node-only types into node-only typings file; 2) produce a minimal edge d.ts and export as `pkg/edge` explicitly; 3) add typesVersions fallback; 4) normalize fetch/http errors in runtime shim; 5) docs snippet about recommended import paths. I'll try PRing a prototype.
